africa's most active volcano erupted a  week ago  in the east of the democratic republic  of congo the city of gomer was flooded  with lava and thousands of people were  forced to flee the area  now they're starting to return looking  for the remains of their former homes  and livelihoods  dw correspondent marielle muller reports  masika francoise lost everything her  house  where she lived with her children and  her family business  the entire life she had carefully built  up over years  consumed by flames in a matter of  seconds  we decided to flee hoping that lava  would not affect our house  but on sunday when we came back  everything was gone  we were left with nothing  she had no time to take any belongings  with her now she's living with friends  but could only take two of her ten  children the others are with neighbors  one here one there  it's making me very sad but there's  nothing i can do about it  like masika over 4 000 people have lost  their homes and their entire livelihoods  some of them found refuge at friends  houses or in nearby towns but now they  depend on humanitarian assistance in  order to survive  why was there no warning for goma about  2 million people live in the area  the director of the goma volcano  observatory says the world bank did not  renew the funding for their work  with no funds they were unable to  monitor the volcano properly  in october the internet was cut off that  caused problems  we have stations that are 150 kilometers  away  but then we didn't have enough fuel to  get there  when the internet is working the  observatory receives data from sensors  on the volcano every four minutes  for seven months that was not the case  everyone knew that we didn't have the  means that we worked with difficulties  but some volcanologists say there were  clear signs that the volcano could erupt  in the next few years  civil society activists say the blame  lies squarely with the government  and the gomer volcano observatory itself  those scientists of overhead if they  weren't corrupted  if they didn't spend money or projects  founded by world bank  they we could prevent the death  right now mazika isn't bothered who is  responsible  all she cares about is trying to rebuild  her life  there is nowhere else i can go there's  only one goma  there's only here for me to stay with my  children  like masika the people of goma live  under the shadow of one of the world's  most dangerous volcanoes  they can only hope the city will be  better prepared by the next time it  erupts
 

